# Migration skill — bugs and gaps

Found scaffolding `tournewzealand.com`, the second site through
`webflow-sanity:migration`. Everything here cost time on this migration and will cost it again
on the next one. Ordered by what hurt most.

The skill is at `~/.claude/skills/webflow-sanity/skills/migration/` — `SKILL.md`,
`references/scaffold.md`, `templates/` (6 files), `migration-scripts/` (the engine).

## 1. The scaffold ships no files — only prose

**The single biggest gap.** §2 says *"Copy the config from a finished migration rather than
writing it"*, and `references/scaffold.md` describes the root scripts, the Wrangler configs, the
JSONC readers and the acceptance checks — **in prose, with nothing to copy.** `templates/`
contains six files and not one of them is a scaffold file.

So §2 is unactionable as written, and every file below had to be authored from scratch, from
prose, with the mistakes that implies:

| Area | Files the skill does not ship |
|---|---|
| Root | `package.json`, `.env.example`, `.gitignore` entries, `wrangler.jsonc`, `wrangler.prod.jsonc` |
| Config readers | `config/parse-project.mjs`, `config/read-project.mjs`, `config/read-project.cjs` |
| Schema | `schema/index.mjs` |
| Site | `web/package.json`, `web/astro.config.mjs`, `web/tsconfig.json`, `web/src/env.d.ts`, `web/src/lib/project.ts`, `web/src/lib/sanity.ts`, `web/src/lib/assets.ts`, `web/public/.assetsignore` |
| Studio | `studio/package.json`, `studio/sanity.config.ts`, `studio/sanity.cli.ts`, `studio/tsconfig.json`, `studio/project-config.ts`, `studio/types/raw-modules.d.ts`, `studio/.env.example` |

**Fix:** ship all of them under `templates/scaffold/`, mirroring the repo layout, and give §2 a
`cp -r` block exactly like the one §0 already has for `migration-scripts/`. Prose describing a
file is not a template.

**This is also why Phase 1 came out incomplete here.** Without the files, "set up the initial
Sanity configuration" has no concrete deliverable, so there was nowhere for the PM to put the
project values and no Studio to point at.

## 2. The engine's own dependencies are undeclared

`migration-scripts/` imports `jsdom` and `jsonc-parser`. Nothing in the skill says so. The
symptom is `npm test` and `npm run sanity:verify` dying with:

```
Error [ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND]: Cannot find package 'jsdom' imported from
  migration-scripts/sanity/html-to-portable-text.mjs
```

**Fix:** name them in §0 beside the `cp -r`, and have them in the shipped root `package.json`.

## 3. §2 leads with a layout the engine does not support

`scaffold.md` opens with *"For an Astro app at the repository root, merge these entries into
package.json"* and treats `web/` as the variant. But **every** engine script hardcodes it:

```js
const WEB = join(ROOT, 'web');
```

`extract-webflow-page.mjs`, `fetch-webflow-assets.mjs`, `localise-webflow-assets.mjs`,
`check-assets.mjs`, `verify-port.mjs`, `check-sitemap.mjs`, `gen-redirects.mjs` — all of them.
Follow the documented primary shape and the engine writes into a `web/` that does not exist,
silently, until a later step cannot find `pages.json`.

**Fix:** state `web/` + `studio/` + root orchestration as **the** layout, or read the app
directory from `project.jsonc`. Do not present a choice the engine cannot honour.

## 4. The doc and the only worked example disagree on where Wrangler configs live

`scaffold.md`: *"keep deploy orchestration and both Wrangler configs at the repository root."*
The reference migration (`sdm`) has them in `web/`.

Root is the better answer — `wrangler` then installs once at the root and
`"$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json"` resolves — but the contradiction has to
go.

## 5. The Cloudflare output layout in §8 is stale

§8 and `scaffold.md` prescribe:

```jsonc
"main": "./dist/_worker.js/index.js",
"assets": {"directory": "./dist", ...}
```

and a `public/.assetsignore` containing `_worker.js` and `_routes.json`.

With **Astro 7 + `@astrojs/cloudflare` 14**, a static build emits none of that:

| Prescribed | Actual |
|---|---|
| `dist/_worker.js/index.js` | does not exist |
| `dist/_routes.json` | does not exist |
| `assets.directory: ./dist` | the site is in **`dist/client/`** |
| — | `dist/server/` exists and is **empty** when everything prerenders |
| — | the adapter writes its own **`dist/client/wrangler.json`** declaring a SESSION KV and an IMAGES binding |

For a fully-prerendered site the correct production config has **no `main` at all** — it is an
assets-only deployment. Copying the prescribed shape yields a Worker entry pointing at a file
that was never written.

**Fix:** stop prescribing paths. Tell the reader to build first and read what the adapter
actually emitted, document the v14 layout, and spell out what changes the moment one route sets
`prerender = false` — add `main` (the adapter's real server entry, read not guessed), drop
`not_found_handling`, and carry over the adapter's bindings.

## 6. `not_found_handling` guidance points at a file that no longer exists

The §8 decision table is still correct in substance, but its verification step is
`cat dist/_routes.json` — which v14 does not produce. The check is now
`grep -rn "prerender = false" web/src/` plus reading `web/dist/client/wrangler.json`.

## 7. Reading `project.jsonc` from site code breaks the prerender

`scaffold.md` says to *"use an ESM filesystem reader anchored to the root command's working
directory"* and warns that *"Astro may relocate bundled modules."* That is not enough to act on.
What actually happens when a page imports the ESM reader:

```
TypeError: The "path" argument must be of type string or an instance of URL. Received undefined
    at fileURLToPath (node-internal:internal_url)
    at web/dist/server/.prerender/chunks/index_DoNnIZDQ.mjs:776
```

`import.meta.url` is `undefined` once Astro bundles the module into a hashed prerender chunk, and
the stack frame points into that chunk rather than at the cause.

**The pattern that works:** resolve `project.jsonc` once in `astro.config.mjs` — plain Node,
never bundled — and inject it with Vite `define`. No filesystem reader ever enters the bundle, so
the failure is structurally impossible rather than merely fixed.

**Fix:** prescribe that, and ship `web/astro.config.mjs` + `web/src/lib/project.ts` as templates.

## 8. The `define` pattern has two traps of its own

Both cost a build cycle here, and both will recur, so they belong in the doc alongside item 7.

> [!WARNING]
> **A local `declare const` is erased along with the export that uses it.** esbuild treats
> `declare const __PROJECT_CONFIG__` as a type-only binding and strips the `export default` that
> references it, failing with the thoroughly misleading
> `[MISSING_EXPORT] "default" is not exported by "src/lib/project.ts"`. Declare it as a
> `declare global { var … }` instead.

> [!WARNING]
> **Vite `define` replaces the BARE identifier only.** `globalThis.__PROJECT_CONFIG__` is a
> member expression, is never matched, and is `undefined` at render time **with no build error
> at all** — the worst available failure mode.

## 9. A fourth asset-URL trap, missing from §5

§5 documents the HTML-vs-CSS terminator and the parentheses-in-filenames trap. There is a third
form on this site: Webflow writes inline backgrounds as

```html
style="background-image:url(&quot;https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/…&quot;)"
```

A scanner that does not **decode entities before matching** runs past the closing `&quot;` and
captures a URL ending in `&quot;)`. Webflow answers those with **403**, so they present as dead
assets rather than as the local bug they are — 127 of them on the first pass here.

**Fix:** add to §5's trap table — decode entities first, *then* apply the per-context terminator.

## 10. There is no definition of "the scaffold is done"

§9's checklist is cut-over oriented. `scaffold.md`'s acceptance list is all commands and no
files, so a scaffold can pass every listed check while the Studio does not exist — which is
exactly what happened.

**Fix:** a §2 acceptance list of **files that must exist**, followed by the commands. Phase 1 is
complete when the PM can fill in two env files and nothing else is missing.

## 11. There is no way to discover the collection ids the pull needs

`wf:pull` imports `COLLECTIONS` from `collections.mjs` — a map of name to Webflow collection id —
so the pull cannot run until the ids are known. **Nothing in the engine produces them**, and §4
opens with `npm run wf:pull` as though it were the first step.

Webflow's `manifest.json` is not a substitute: it names only the collections that have a DETAIL
TEMPLATE. On this site that is 6 of 13. The other seven appear only inside a list on another page
— `faq`, `island`, `pickUp`, `special`, `featured`, `alert`, `cancellationPolicy` — and are
exactly the ones that get missed.

**Fix:** ship a discovery script. Written here as
`migration-scripts/webflow/list-collections.mjs`; it needs no configuration at all (site id from
`project.jsonc`, token through `lib/env.mjs`), which is the point, since it runs before there is
any. `--fields` dumps each collection's field schema, which is what `FIELD_MAP` is authored from.

## 12. "Which rich-text field does the site actually render?" needs a stated method

§4 says a reference site had *"two rich-text fields where the site renders the second"* but not how
to tell. The obvious method — look for the field's value in the item's own detail page — **reports
live fields as dead**, because a summary field typically renders on listing cards somewhere else
entirely. On this site `tourCategory.brief` renders in the nav on *all 94* pages and on its own
category page not at all.

**Fix:** state the method. Search every archived page, not the item's own; normalise `&nbsp;`,
` `, zero-width characters, curly quotes and en/em dashes on both sides before comparing; and
report *where* each field was found, because the location is the evidence for what the field is
for. That is also what distinguishes a genuinely dead field —
`tour.pricing-section-intro-text` here, populated on four live tours and rendered nowhere.

## 13. The shipped engine has nine defects that each cost a debugging cycle

These are not documentation gaps — they are bugs in `migration-scripts/`, found by running it.
All nine are fixed in this repo and the fixes should go back to the skill.

### 13.1 `SHELL` cannot express a multi-part shell

`pages.config.mjs` takes ONE `{tag, cls}` for the nav and one for the footer, and
`extract-webflow-page.mjs` calls `findSpan` once for each. This site's header is four sibling
blocks inside an unclassed wrapper, and its footer is three siblings whose composition varies by
template. Neither is expressible. **Fix:** accept an array and take the contiguous range from the
first matched part to the last. Matching a common ancestor instead is wrong — on a `/tour/*` page
the nav's outermost ancestor is `.page-wrapper`, i.e. the whole page.

### 13.2 `data-wf-page-id` makes the shared shell page-specific

Webflow stamps the current page's id onto every `<form>`, including forms inside the nav and
footer. The nav fragments were otherwise byte-identical at 43735 bytes each, and the extract
aborted with "nav differs from the shared nav.html". **Fix:** `stripCurrent` must replace it with
a marker the route substitutes per page — the same treatment `w--current` already gets.

### 13.3 The shared-shell comparison must ignore ALL whitespace

Webflow indents by nesting depth, so a template whose shell sits one level deeper emits identical
furniture with every line shifted — 994 bytes of indentation here. And `>\s+<` is not enough: the
shell contains an embedded `<style>` block whose CSS indentation also shifts, so the comparison
still failed 21349 bytes in with both sides looking identical on screen. **Fix:** compare on
`replace(/\s+/g, ' ')`. Safe, because the verifier compares visible text and tag counts, not bytes.

### 13.4 `<body>` attributes are never captured, and they are load-bearing

The extractor records `lang` and `data-wf-page` from `<html>` and nothing from `<body>`. This site
has three variants across 94 pages: bare, `data-ix="full-opacity-on-load"`, and one page carrying
`order="payment"` that its own JS dispatches on. Hardcoding one variant would add an IX2
opacity animation to the 69 pages without it — and IX2 animates opacity **from 0**, so those pages
render blank if the runtime ever fails — while dropping the attribute the payment flow needs.
**Fix:** capture `<body>`'s attributes per page into the manifest.

### 13.5 Webflow's `preconnect` survives the port and fails `assets:check`

`<link rel="preconnect" href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com">` carries no path, so the
localiser — which matches a host followed by `/` — never sees it. It reaches every built page and
`assets:check` then fails all 23 of them on a bare host with nothing to rewrite, which reads as an
unlocalised asset. **Fix:** drop `preconnect`/`dns-prefetch` to Webflow hosts during extraction.

### 13.6 `localise:assets` truncates parenthesised filenames — again

The skill documents this trap and the shipped code still has it, in the CSS branch:
`[^"')\s]+` stops at the `)` inside `photo%20(1).webp`. Nor can a lazy match with a lookahead fix
it — it halts at the same paren. Separately the HTML branch does not stop at an **entity-encoded**
quote, so `style="background-image:url(&quot;…&quot;)"` yields a URL ending in `&quot;)`. Both
report as 403s from Webflow rather than as local bugs: 127 of them on the first pass here.
**Fix:** one pattern that stops at a literal quote, whitespace, `<`/`>`, or an entity-encoded
quote. Webflow quotes every `url()` it emits — 117 of 117 in this stylesheet — so the CSS branch
needs no paren handling at all.

> [!CAUTION]
> **The first, buggy pass rewrites files in place**, so the truncated paths are then baked into
> `src/html` and the ported CSS and re-running the fixed localiser cannot repair them. Recovery is
> re-extract plus re-fetch the stylesheet. Worth a warning in §5.

### 13.7 `check-assets` truncates the same filenames, and fails a site with no self-hosted fonts

Its `LOCAL_REF` pattern has the identical `[^"')]+` bug, so it reported six files as missing that
were present on disk. And the font check fails on `fontRefs.size === 0`, which cannot pass on a
site whose type comes from Google Fonts — a permanently red check is a check nobody reads.
**Fix:** quote-terminated branches for `url()`; and distinguish "no CSS was found" (the port
failed) from "CSS declares no font file" (a webfont service, external by design).

### 13.8 `verify-port` does not decode entities, so an apostrophe fails the page

Webflow writes `&#x27;`, Astro writes `&#39;` — the same character, two spellings. The verifier
compares visible TEXT and must not see a difference. `/great-walks` failed on its own title while
its tag counts matched exactly, 596 to 596. **Fix:** decode numeric and named entities on both
sides before comparing.

### 13.9 Three scripts hardcode `web/dist`, which the adapter moved

`verify-port`, `check-assets` and `check-sitemap` all read `web/dist`. `@astrojs/cloudflare` v14
writes the site to `web/dist/client`. All three reported "run npm run build first" against a build
that had just succeeded — a failure that reads as a missing step rather than a stale path.
**Fix:** resolve it once in a shared `lib/paths.mjs`, probing for `dist/client`.

## 14. Three of the shipped TESTS cannot pass

Worse than a bug in the engine: a test that fails on correct output teaches everyone to ignore it.

### 14.1 `test-converter`: the invisible characters were lost from the source

The nbsp assertion reads `if (/&nbsp;| /.test(sourceAll))` and
`!portableTextToPlain(all).includes(' ')` — and both of those are a **plain space, 0x20**, not
U+00A0. So the guard matches any text containing a space and the assertion is false for
essentially all prose: it fails on every site, always, reporting a converter bug that does not
exist. The U+200C assertion beside it has the same shape. **Fix:** write invisible characters as
` ` / `‌` escapes so a transcription cannot silently change what is asserted.

### 14.2 `test-portable`: the nested-list assertion forbids markup

`/<li>[^<]*<(ul|ol)\b/` additionally demands no markup between the `<li>` and the nested list, so
a correctly nested list fails the moment its parent item contains a `<strong>` — which real
content always does. **Fix:** `/<li>(?:(?!<\/li>)[\s\S])*?<(ul|ol)\b/`.

### 14.3 `verify-lib`: `sig()` is applied to both sides and only one is HTML

`compareBody` runs the same signature over the Webflow source and the converted Portable Text.
`sig` strips `<[^>]+>` before decoding entities — right for HTML, destructive for text. This
site's copy contains `&lt; Option 1 : Halfday Rotorua sightseeing Tour &gt;`: on the HTML side the
brackets survive tag-stripping and decode to `<Option 1…>`, on the converted side they arrive
already decoded and are deleted as a tag. The verifier reported a divergence in a field the
converter handled perfectly, and the "fix" would have been to break the converter. **Fix:** a
separate `sigText` for the already-decoded side.

## 15. The per-site `verify.mjs` template teaches a broken GROQ idiom

Its reference check is shaped like
`count(categories[^._ref in *[_type=="x"]._id]) != count(categories)`. `^` does not reach the
array element there, so the inner filter matches nothing and **every** document counts as broken —
29 of 29 tours and 9 of 9 reviews here. A reference check that fails on everything is the tell.
**Fix:** dereference and compare lengths — `count(categories) != count(categories[]->_id)`.

## 16. Reference fields point at non-live items, and nothing in §4 mentions it

Webflow does not clean up references when an item is archived. 21 `relatedTours` and review
`tours` entries here name tours that are archived or draft. Converted as-is they are dangling
references, which import without complaint and surface later as a blank card or a "related tour"
link to a 404 — they are already broken on the live site.

**Fix:** §4 should say to build the set of live ids per collection FIRST, prune reference arrays
against it, and report every pruned entry. Then assert in `verify.mjs` that no document lost its
last category or area in the process, because a tour with neither falls out of every listing on
the site.

## 17. Smaller things

- **Per-site templates crash instead of refusing.** `templates/verify.mjs` and
  `templates/collections.mjs` ship with `<parentCollection>` placeholders, so `npm run
  sanity:verify` dies with `ENOENT: migration/raw/<parentCollection>.json`. The fixtures check
  gets this right — *"Create it and add verbatim excerpts…"* — and these should match it.
- **npm 11 blocks postinstall scripts by default.** `esbuild` and `workerd` are gated, so
  `wrangler` has no binary until `npm install-scripts approve esbuild workerd` is run, in each
  package that depends on them. Worth one line in §2.
- **`.env` location is load-bearing and only explained in the reference repo's comments.** With
  the app in `web/`, `.env` stays at the ROOT and `web/astro.config.mjs` needs
  `vite.envDir: '..'`, aligned with the guard's `--env-dir`. Get it wrong and the build succeeds
  while every query reads the wrong dataset. `scaffold.md` mentions the setting but not the
  consequence.
- **`studioHost` availability is not verifiable up front.** `navi.sanity.studio` already 302s to
  a studio under another org id, and `sanity deploy --dry-run` only says *"name availability is
  checked on deploy"*. Worth a §2 note to check the hostname early, since it cannot be confirmed
  without deploying.
- **`sanity deploy --dry-run` exists and is genuinely non-publishing** on CLI 8.29. §2's
  acceptance hedges on this; it can be stated.
